Also, there'll be cases where yachts have been dropped out of the slings.

235
00:16:58,081 --> 00:17:01,120
Dockwise takes a different approach to moving yachts.

236
00:17:01,121 --> 00:17:03,680
They flood the transport ship.

237
00:17:03,681 --> 00:17:09,920
This ship is a semi-submersible ship, which means that everything under the ship is either

238
00:17:09,921 --> 00:17:13,960
engine room or fuel tanks or water ballast tanks.

239
00:17:13,961 --> 00:17:19,000
All the cargo is carried on top of the deck, and the ship will actually submerge below

240
00:17:19,001 --> 00:17:22,840
the water and the yachts are loaded from the stern.

241
00:17:22,841 --> 00:17:28,840
They drive into the ship just as if they're driving into a marina.

242
00:17:28,841 --> 00:17:30,480
It may seem simple enough.

243
00:17:30,481 --> 00:17:34,300
but plenty of work and planning is required so the yachts arrive in one piece.

244
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,400
First, up to six months prior to the voyage,

245
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:44,560
owners send dockwise details about their yachts, including size and destination.

246
00:17:45,680 --> 00:17:49,060
From that data, the loadmaster draws up a loading plan.

247
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:54,600
When the day to load arrives, any miscalculation could delay the shipment,

248
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
or worse, damage the fragile cargo.

249
00:17:57,960 --> 00:18:01,780
The shipping cost starts at around $60 per square foot of yacht.

250
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:08,060
Of course, that $60 multiplies fast when many yachts top 3,000 square feet.

251
00:18:09,220 --> 00:18:12,320
The day modern marvels arrived in Vancouver, Canada,

252
00:18:12,321 --> 00:18:16,020
the area was suffering through its worst rain and snowstorm in years.

253
00:18:16,640 --> 00:18:19,260
But that didn't deter Dockwise from completing the job.

254
00:18:20,060 --> 00:18:23,860
Any rain or anything like that is not really a consideration for the operation.

255
00:18:23,861 --> 00:18:31,100
first the ballast tanks fill with seawater direct from the harbor for about six hours

256
00:18:31,101 --> 00:18:36,220
once that process is complete the water level and deck level are the same

257
00:18:36,221 --> 00:18:42,340
after a second final ballasting the deck is completely submerged and the yachts can come

258
00:18:42,341 --> 00:18:49,300
aboard depending on the yachts being loaded the ship's deck can be anywhere between six

259
00:18:49,301 --> 00:18:51,160
and 16 feet underwater.

260
00:18:52,300 --> 00:18:54,780
Please enter bow first, tie up port side alongside.

261
00:18:56,140 --> 00:18:58,300
Loading the yacht takes constant communication

262
00:18:58,301 --> 00:19:00,560
between yacht crews and the dockwise workers.

263
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:05,800
After hours of careful loading, the yachts are on deck,

264
00:19:06,220 --> 00:19:07,940
but the work is far from over.

265
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:10,360
The yachts still need to be secured

266
00:19:10,361 --> 00:19:12,620
against any rough waves they'll encounter at sea.

267
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:16,020
That's where the scuba divers take over.

268
00:19:19,300 --> 00:19:24,300
We still have to go forward, now it's 1.5 feet.

269
00:19:24,301 --> 00:19:28,300
The divers set up temporary supports beneath each yacht.

270
00:19:28,301 --> 00:19:32,300
Row by row, they work from the front to the rear of the ship.

271
00:19:32,301 --> 00:19:38,300
At the same time, water slowly drains from the ship's deck.

272
00:19:38,301 --> 00:19:42,300
Once the deck is dry and all the yachts are standing on their temporary supports,

273
00:19:42,301 --> 00:19:45,300
then the crew will come and install sea fastings,

274
00:19:45,301 --> 00:19:47,700
which are metal bracings with rubber pads,

275
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,540
which are affixed to the frames of the yachts

276
00:19:50,541 --> 00:19:51,980
and welded to the deck of the ship.

277
00:19:52,640 --> 00:19:55,460
Those will secure the yachts for all the forces

278
00:19:55,461 --> 00:19:56,900
they will have when the ship is at sea.

279
00:19:58,660 --> 00:20:00,580
Nearly two days pass from the time

280
00:20:00,581 --> 00:20:02,040
the ballast tanks begin to fill

281
00:20:02,041 --> 00:20:05,120
