00:24:25,840 --> 00:24:27,160
Welcome to Netflix,

354
00:24:27,740 --> 00:24:30,120
a company that delivers movies straight to your mailbox.

355
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:34,160
Just log into their website and create a list of movies.

356
00:24:34,840 --> 00:24:37,080
The first available DVD on the list ships,

357
00:24:37,400 --> 00:24:39,300
and in most cases will arrive the next day.

358
00:24:40,860 --> 00:24:44,100
But a lot takes place behind the scenes to make that happen.

359
00:24:45,340 --> 00:24:49,940
First, workers wheel out nearly a million DVDs from storage to sorters,

360
00:24:49,941 --> 00:24:51,940
which scan each one.

361
00:24:53,940 --> 00:24:58,940
What you're seeing now is what we call our Scan Again Tomorrow process, or SAT process.

362
00:24:58,941 --> 00:25:04,940
Its basic purpose is to sort all the DVDs that we have in the facility into two piles,

363
00:25:04,941 --> 00:25:07,940
one that have orders and one that do not have orders.

364
00:25:07,941 --> 00:25:11,940
The machines are modified mail sorting equipment.

365
00:25:11,941 --> 00:25:15,940
But Netflix added rubber stoppers and custom engineered each machine

366
00:25:15,941 --> 00:25:21,080
to make them perfectly straight, to avoid damaging or bending the DVDs during the process.

367
00:25:23,180 --> 00:25:29,700
With each sorter capable of scanning 30,000 DVDs in an hour and 10 sorting lines operating at once,

368
00:25:30,440 --> 00:25:32,960
scanning the inventory takes just over three hours.

369
00:25:33,900 --> 00:25:37,220
But Netflix didn't always have this speedy process at its disposal.

370
00:25:39,400 --> 00:25:41,540
Netflix had some pretty humble roots.

371
00:25:41,980 --> 00:25:44,280
It wasn't always automated as it is here today.

372
00:25:44,281 --> 00:25:48,280
In fact, all these processes once upon a time were manual.

373
00:25:48,281 --> 00:25:52,280
The operator would scan a disk to see if it had an order or didn't have an order.

374
00:25:52,281 --> 00:25:57,280
Well, back in 1999, we had only a few thousand titles, and a big shipping day for us would

375
00:25:57,281 --> 00:26:00,280
have been to ship over a couple thousand DVDs.

376
00:26:00,281 --> 00:26:05,280
Nowadays, we have over 40 million DVDs in stock, over 100,000 titles, and we'll ship

377
00:26:05,281 --> 00:26:09,280
well over 2 million DVDs in any given day.

378
00:26:09,281 --> 00:26:13,280
Once sorting is complete, DVDs without an order return to storage.

379
00:26:13,281 --> 00:26:17,660
